  "d9db375f-3f20-4002-8822-d99a842f5780": {
   "id": "d9db375f-3f20-4002-8822-d99a842f5780",
   "message": {
    "id": "d9db375f-3f20-4002-8822-d99a842f5780",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1774582889.861,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "Short answer: don't report the mean of pairwise Cohen's kappas as if it were a single multi-rater statistic without saying so. Two clean options:\n\n**Option A: Fleiss' kappa (or Krippendorff's alpha)**\nBoth are designed for >2 raters. If every one of the 412 windows was labeled by all three of you, Fleiss' kappa is the textbook choice. Krippendorff's alpha (nominal) is more robust if any window is missing a label and is increasingly what HCI reviewers expect.\n\n**Option B: Mean pairwise Cohen's kappa (Light's kappa)**\nAveraging pairwise Cohen's kappas is a known statistic, *Light's kappa*. That is exactly what you computed: (0.74 + 0.69 + 0.70) / 3 = 0.71. It's legitimate as long as you name it.\n\nSuggested sentence:\n\n> Three authors independently labeled a stratified sample of 412 windows. Agreement was substantial (Light's κ = 0.71; pairwise Cohen's κ = 0.69–0.74).\n\nIf you have time, compute Krippendorff's alpha too and put it in a footnote. It will land very close to 0.71 if your marginals are similar across raters.\n\nWant the code for alpha from your label CSV?"
     ]
    },
