    "create_time": 1774583220.434,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "Yes, and it helps you. Reviewers trust a paper more when it shows where agreement breaks down.\n\nReport per-class agreement as one-vs-rest kappa (or per-class F1 between raters) in a small table:\n\n| State | one-vs-rest κ |\n|---|---|\n| productive | … |\n| stalled-after-error | … |\n| help-seeking | … |\n| off-task | … |\n| idle | … |\n\nThen one sentence of interpretation, e.g.: *\"Disagreement concentrated in stalled-after-error vs. productive, which differ mainly in whether the student's next edit addresses the error; we resolved these by discussion and refined the guide (v3).\"*\n\nThat ties the reliability number to the labeling guide revision, which is the story you already have."
     ]
    },
